<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Song of the Albigensian Crusade</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Canso de la Crotzada</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a> is an Occitan epic poem of nearly 10,000 lines describing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catharism">Cathars</a> from 1208 to 1219. The first part, by William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudela">Tudela</a>, favours the crusaders; an anonymous continuator wrote the rest from the side of the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, with vivid accounts of the sieges of Toulouse and the death of Simon de Montfort.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/song-of-the-albigensian-crusade.jpg" alt="Song of the Albigensian Crusade" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_VIII_Marmande.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The poem is modelled on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-french">Old French</a> chanson de geste and narrates events from March 1208 to June 1219. William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudela">Tudela</a>, who names himself maestre W. in the first and ninth laisses, gives 1210 as the year he began, and wrote his part towards 1213. The author of the second part is not known for certain; Saverio Guida has proposed the troubadour Gui de Cavalhon, a noble and one of the most faithful allies of Raymond VI of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>. That author was apparently an educated man with some knowledge of theology and law, who belonged to the diocese of Toulouse and called Bishop Folquet de Marselha our bishop.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>William's part takes up the first 2749 lines, in 130 laisses, rhymed stanzas of varying length, and brings the story to the beginning of 1213. It is strongly partisan against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catharism">Cathars</a> and the southerners in general, and it contains the earliest use of the term <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>, as crozada. The second part, laisses 131 to 214, runs to 6811 lines and covers events from 1213 onwards from the opposite side, critical of the crusaders and strongly for the southerners, though not for Catharism. It mentions the death of Guy of Montfort, which took place in 1228; whether the whole part was written after that date, or the reference was inserted later, is uncertain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> survives in a single <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, fr. 25425 in the Bibliothèque nationale, written in or around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> about 1275. It is one of three major contemporary narratives of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>, with the Historia Albigensis of Pierre des Vaux-de-Cernay and the Chronica of William of Puylaurens, and the only major one that takes the southern view. It matters most to historians for April 1216 to June 1219, when Pierre's account grows sketchy. Claude Charles Fauriel first edited the text in 1837; Paul Meyer published the first critical edition with a French translation in 1875–1879, Eugène Martin-Chabot another in 1931–1961, and Shirley an English translation in 1996.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Song of the Albigensian Crusade” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Occitan epic <b>Song of the Albigensian Crusade</b> and the memory of the Cathars later became central to regional identity in Languedoc.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
